"use strict";(self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith=self.webpackChunkdatabricks_monolith||[]).push([[24228],{501847:n=>{n.exports="# How to SQL authoring (detection logic)\n\nThis guide provides patterns for writing SQL that detects security threats and anomalous behavior.\n\nDetection SQL queries identify patterns in event data that indicate potential security incidents. Each query you write will generate Notable events (one per row returned) for security analysts to investigate.\n\n**Common Use Cases:**\n\n> _\"Alert when a user logs in from two different countries within an hour\"_\n\n> _\"Detect when more than 100 events come from a single IP in 5 minutes\"_\n\n> _\"Find users who accessed files they've never accessed before\"_\n\n> _\"Write SQL to detect after-hours VPN connections\"_\n\n---\n\n## Overview\n\nThis skill focuses on SQL patterns for scheduled batch detections. When streaming input is enabled and explicitly requested, follow the page instructions for the streaming rule shape and write streaming SQL against the `input` dataframe; do not force batch input. Use `info-schedule` when the user asks about scheduling, compute resources, or execution latency trade-offs.\n\n---\n\n## IMPORTANT: SQL Validation Workflow\n\n**Before proposing any SQL query to the user, you MUST:**\n\n1. **Generate the SQL** based on patterns in this skill\n2. **Execute the SQL** using `executeSql` to validate it works\n3. **Check for errors:**\n   - If syntax errors → Fix the SQL and re-run\n   - If field not found → Use table discovery tools to find correct field names, update SQL, re-run\n   - If no results → Verify time window has data, adjust if needed\n4. **Show sample results** to the user (e.g., \"This query returned 5 rows...\")\n5. **Only then propose** the validated SQL to update the detection rule\n\n**NEVER propose SQL queries that you haven't successfully executed and validated.**\n\nIf you encounter errors during validation:\n\n- Use `tableSearch` to find correct table names\n- Use `readTable` to verify field names exist\n- Adjust SQL syntax, field names, or filters\n- Re-run until the query executes successfully\n- Explain what you fixed when presenting to the user\n\n---\n\n## Core Detection Patterns\n\nDetection SQL queries typically follow one of these patterns, or combine multiple patterns together.\n\n### 1. Threshold-Based Detection\n\nIdentify events that exceed fixed conditions within a time window.\n\n**Use for:** Known suspicious conditions (brute force, large data transfers, repeated violations)\n\n**Pattern Template:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  entity_field,           -- User, IP, host, etc.\n  time_bucket,            -- Aggregation time period\n  COUNT(*) as event_count -- Or SUM(), AVG(), etc.\nFROM gold.your_table\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND filter_conditions  -- Status, action, etc.\nGROUP BY entity_field, time_bucket\nHAVING event_count > threshold_value\nORDER BY event_count DESC\n```\n\n**Key Elements:**\n\n- **Time filter:** Always include explicit time range\n- **Aggregation:** GROUP BY relevant dimensions (user, IP, time period)\n- **Threshold:** Use HAVING clause on aggregated values\n- **Sorting:** ORDER BY for prioritization\n\n**Example: Brute Force Login Detection**\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  src_endpoint.ip AS source_ip,\n  DATE_TRUNC('hour', time) AS event_hour,\n  COALESCE(http_request.user_agent, 'unknown') AS user_agent,\n  COUNT(*) AS failed_login_count\nFROM gold.authentication\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND status = 'Failure'\n  AND activity_name = 'Logon'\nGROUP BY src_endpoint.ip, DATE_TRUNC('hour', time), http_request.user_agent\nHAVING failed_login_count > 100\nORDER BY failed_login_count DESC\n```\n\n**Note:** Verify that fields like `src_endpoint.ip`, `status`, and `activity_name` exist in your specific Gold table schema.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Time-Series Anomaly Detection\n\nCompare current metrics to historical baselines to detect statistical deviations.\n\n**Use for:** Identifying abnormal patterns without fixed thresholds (unusual login counts, traffic spikes)\n\n**Pattern Template:**\n\n```sql\nWITH historical_and_current AS (\n  SELECT\n    entity_field,\n    time_bucket,\n    metric_value,\n    -- Label data as current or historical\n    CASE\n      WHEN time_bucket = target_period THEN true\n      ELSE false\n    END AS is_current\n  FROM gold.your_table\n  WHERE time BETWEEN baseline_start AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  GROUP BY entity_field, time_bucket\n),\nstatistics AS (\n  SELECT\n    entity_field,\n    MAX(CASE WHEN is_current THEN metric_value END) AS current_value,\n    AVG(CASE WHEN NOT is_current THEN metric_value END) AS hist_avg,\n    STDDEV(CASE WHEN NOT is_current THEN metric_value END) AS hist_stddev,\n    PERCENTILE(CASE WHEN NOT is_current THEN metric_value END, 0.95) AS hist_p95,\n    -- Z-score calculation\n    (MAX(CASE WHEN is_current THEN metric_value END) - AVG(CASE WHEN NOT is_current THEN metric_value END))\n      / NULLIF(STDDEV(CASE WHEN NOT is_current THEN metric_value END), 0) AS z_score\n  FROM historical_and_current\n  GROUP BY entity_field\n)\nSELECT\n  entity_field,\n  current_value,\n  hist_avg,\n  z_score,\n  CASE\n    WHEN z_score > 3.0 THEN 'anomaly_zscore'\n    WHEN current_value > hist_avg + 3.0 * hist_stddev THEN 'anomaly_stdev'\n    WHEN current_value > hist_p95 THEN 'anomaly_percentile'\n  END AS anomaly_type\nFROM statistics\nWHERE anomaly_type IS NOT NULL\n```\n\n**Key Elements:**\n\n- **Baseline period:** 30+ days of historical data\n- **Current period:** Yesterday, last hour, etc.\n- **Statistical methods:** Z-score, standard deviation, percentile\n- **Minimum samples:** Require sufficient historical data (10+ data points)\n\n**Example: Unusual Login Volume**\n\n```sql\nWITH daily_logins AS (\n  SELECT\n    actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    DATE_TRUNC('day', time) AS login_date,\n    COUNT(*) AS login_count\n  FROM gold.authentication\n  WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 31 DAYS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n    AND activity_name = 'Logon'\n  GROUP BY actor.user.name, DATE_TRUNC('day', time)\n),\nlabeled_data AS (\n  SELECT\n    user_name,\n    login_count,\n    login_date = DATE_TRUNC('day', CURRENT_DATE() - INTERVAL 1 DAY) AS is_yesterday,\n    login_date < DATE_TRUNC('day', CURRENT_DATE() - INTERVAL 1 DAY) AS is_historical\n  FROM daily_logins\n)\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  MAX(CASE WHEN is_yesterday THEN login_count END) AS yesterday_logins,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN is_historical THEN login_count END) AS avg_logins_30d,\n  (MAX(CASE WHEN is_yesterday THEN login_count END) - AVG(CASE WHEN is_historical THEN login_count END))\n    / NULLIF(STDDEV(CASE WHEN is_historical THEN login_count END), 0) AS z_score\nFROM labeled_data\nGROUP BY user_name\nHAVING z_score > 3.0\n  AND COUNT(CASE WHEN is_historical THEN 1 END) >= 10  -- Require 10+ days of history\n```\n\n**Note:** Verify field names in your `gold.authentication` table.\n\n---\n\n### 3. First-Time-Seen Detection\n\nFlag new entity relationships not previously observed.\n\n**Use for:** Detecting lateral movement, new access patterns, first-time resource access\n\n**Pattern Template:**\n\n```sql\n-- This pattern requires a persistent lookup table\n-- See the first-time-seen skill for complete setup instructions\n\nSELECT\n  entity_field,\n  target_field,\n  first_seen_time\nFROM lookup_table\nWHERE first_seen_time >= CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS\nORDER BY first_seen_time DESC\n```\n\n**Key Elements:**\n\n- **Lookup table:** Persistent cache of known entity-target pairs\n- **Baseline population:** Initial load from historical data (30-180 days)\n- **Detection window:** Check for relationships first seen in last 24 hours\n- **Update mechanism:** Continuously refresh lookup table\n\n**Note:** This pattern is complex and requires table management. See the **first-time-seen skill** for detailed implementation guidance.\n\n---\n\n### 4. Geo-Anomaly Detection\n\nDetect impossible travel or suspicious geographic patterns.\n\n**Use for:** User traveling between distant locations too quickly, access from unusual countries\n\n#### **Approach A: Multiple Locations in Short Time**\n\n```sql\nWITH user_locations AS (\n  SELECT\n    actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    cloud.region AS region,\n    time AS login_time\n  FROM gold.authentication\n  WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n    AND activity_name = 'Logon'\n    AND status = 'Success'\n)\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT region) AS distinct_regions,\n  COLLECT_SET(region) AS regions_accessed,\n  MIN(login_time) AS first_login,\n  MAX(login_time) AS last_login\nFROM user_locations\nGROUP BY user_name\nHAVING distinct_regions >= 2  -- User logged in from 2+ regions\n```\n\n**Note:** Verify that your table has `cloud.region` or equivalent geo fields (e.g., `src_endpoint.location.country`, `geo.country_code`).\n\n#### **Approach B: Impossible Travel (Distance + Time)**\n\n```sql\nWITH login_pairs AS (\n  SELECT\n    a.actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    a.src_endpoint.location.country AS location_a,\n    a.src_endpoint.location.lat AS lat_a,\n    a.src_endpoint.location.lon AS lon_a,\n    a.time AS time_a,\n    b.src_endpoint.location.country AS location_b,\n    b.src_endpoint.location.lat AS lat_b,\n    b.src_endpoint.location.lon AS lon_b,\n    b.time AS time_b,\n    TIMESTAMPDIFF(MINUTE, a.time, b.time) AS time_diff_minutes\n  FROM gold.authentication a\n  JOIN gold.authentication b\n    ON a.actor.user.name = b.actor.user.name\n    AND a.time < b.time\n    AND b.time <= a.time + INTERVAL 4 HOURS  -- Look within 4-hour window\n  WHERE a.time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n    AND a.activity_name = 'Logon'\n    AND b.activity_name = 'Logon'\n    AND a.src_endpoint.location.country != b.src_endpoint.location.country  -- Different countries\n)\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  location_a,\n  location_b,\n  time_diff_minutes,\n  -- Haversine distance formula (approximate km between coordinates)\n  2 * 6371 * ASIN(SQRT(\n    POW(SIN(RADIANS(lat_b - lat_a) / 2), 2) +\n    COS(RADIANS(lat_a)) * COS(RADIANS(lat_b)) *\n    POW(SIN(RADIANS(lon_b - lon_a) / 2), 2)\n  )) AS distance_km,\n  -- Speed in km/h\n  (2 * 6371 * ASIN(SQRT(\n    POW(SIN(RADIANS(lat_b - lat_a) / 2), 2) +\n    COS(RADIANS(lat_a)) * COS(RADIANS(lat_b)) *\n    POW(SIN(RADIANS(lon_b - lon_a) / 2), 2)\n  ))) / (time_diff_minutes / 60.0) AS speed_kmh\nFROM login_pairs\nWHERE speed_kmh > 800  -- Threshold: faster than commercial airline\n```\n\n**Note:** Requires geolocation data (latitude/longitude or country codes) in your authentication table. Verify field names match your schema.\n\n---\n\n### 5. Time-Based Filtering\n\nDetect activity outside normal business hours or specific time patterns.\n\n**Use for:** After-hours access, weekend activity, unusual time-of-day patterns\n\n**Pattern Template:**\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  entity_field,\n  time,\n  HOUR(time) AS hour_of_day,\n  DAYOFWEEK(time) AS day_of_week\nFROM gold.your_table\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND (\n    -- After hours (outside 8 AM - 6 PM)\n    HOUR(time) NOT BETWEEN 8 AND 18\n    OR\n    -- Weekend (Sunday=1, Saturday=7 in Spark SQL)\n    DAYOFWEEK(time) IN (1, 7)\n  )\n```\n\n**Example: After-Hours VPN Connections**\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  src_endpoint.ip AS source_ip,\n  time AS connection_time,\n  HOUR(time) AS hour_of_day,\n  DAYOFWEEK(time) AS day_of_week,\n  CASE\n    WHEN DAYOFWEEK(time) IN (1, 7) THEN 'Weekend'\n    WHEN HOUR(time) < 6 OR HOUR(time) > 22 THEN 'Night'\n    WHEN HOUR(time) < 8 OR HOUR(time) > 18 THEN 'After Hours'\n    ELSE 'Business Hours'\n  END AS time_category\nFROM gold.vpn_activity\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND activity_name = 'VPN Connect'\n  AND (\n    HOUR(time) NOT BETWEEN 8 AND 18  -- Outside 8 AM - 6 PM\n    OR DAYOFWEEK(time) IN (1, 7)     -- Weekend\n  )\nORDER BY time DESC\n```\n\n**Note:** Time-based detection should consider your organization's work schedule and time zones. Adjust hour ranges accordingly.\n\n---\n\n## Combining Patterns\n\nMany effective detections combine multiple patterns. Here are common combinations:\n\n### Threshold + Time-Based\n\nDetect high volumes of activity during unusual hours.\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  DATE_TRUNC('hour', time) AS event_hour,\n  COUNT(*) AS event_count,\n  CASE\n    WHEN HOUR(time) NOT BETWEEN 8 AND 18 THEN 'After Hours'\n    ELSE 'Business Hours'\n  END AS time_category\nFROM gold.file_activity\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND action = 'Download'\n  AND HOUR(time) NOT BETWEEN 8 AND 18  -- After hours only\nGROUP BY actor.user.name, DATE_TRUNC('hour', time)\nHAVING event_count > 50  -- High volume threshold\n```\n\n### Threshold + Geo-Anomaly\n\nDetect high-volume activity from unusual locations.\n\n```sql\nWITH user_countries AS (\n  SELECT\n    actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    src_endpoint.location.country AS country,\n    COUNT(*) AS event_count\n  FROM gold.authentication\n  WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  GROUP BY actor.user.name, src_endpoint.location.country\n)\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT country) AS country_count,\n  SUM(event_count) AS total_events,\n  COLLECT_SET(country) AS countries\nFROM user_countries\nGROUP BY user_name\nHAVING country_count >= 2    -- Multiple countries\n  AND total_events > 100     -- High volume\n```\n\n### Anomaly + Time-Based\n\nDetect statistical anomalies that occur during specific time periods.\n\n```sql\nWITH hourly_activity AS (\n  SELECT\n    actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    DATE_TRUNC('hour', time) AS hour_bucket,\n    HOUR(time) AS hour_of_day,\n    COUNT(*) AS activity_count\n  FROM gold.file_activity\n  WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 7 DAYS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  GROUP BY actor.user.name, DATE_TRUNC('hour', time)\n),\nlabeled_data AS (\n  SELECT\n    user_name,\n    activity_count,\n    hour_of_day,\n    hour_bucket >= DATE_TRUNC('hour', CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 DAY) AS is_recent\n  FROM hourly_activity\n)\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  hour_of_day,\n  MAX(CASE WHEN is_recent THEN activity_count END) AS recent_count,\n  AVG(CASE WHEN NOT is_recent THEN activity_count END) AS historical_avg,\n  (MAX(CASE WHEN is_recent THEN activity_count END) - AVG(CASE WHEN NOT is_recent THEN activity_count END))\n    / NULLIF(STDDEV(CASE WHEN NOT is_recent THEN activity_count END), 0) AS z_score\nFROM labeled_data\nWHERE hour_of_day NOT BETWEEN 8 AND 18  -- After hours only\nGROUP BY user_name, hour_of_day\nHAVING z_score > 2.0  -- Lower threshold for after-hours activity\n```\n\n---\n\n## Building Custom Detection Patterns\n\nWhen templates don't fit your use case, follow this framework to build custom detection SQL:\n\n### Step 1: Define Detection Logic\n\n**Ask:**\n\n- What behavior am I trying to detect?\n- What makes this behavior suspicious?\n- What entities are involved? (user, IP, host, file, etc.)\n- What time window matters?\n\n**Example:** \"Detect users downloading sensitive files from unusual browsers\"\n\n### Step 2: Identify Data Requirements\n\n**Determine:**\n\n- Which Gold table(s) contain the relevant events?\n- What fields do I need? (user, action, file_path, user_agent, time)\n- What filters narrow down to suspicious events?\n\n**Example:**\n\n- Table: `gold.file_activity`\n- Fields: `actor.user.name`, `file_path`, `http_request.user_agent`, `action`, `time`\n- Filters: `action = 'Download'` AND `file_path LIKE '%confidential%'`\n\n### Step 3: Choose Base Pattern\n\n**Select:**\n\n- Threshold: if you're counting events\n- Anomaly: if comparing to historical baseline\n- First-time-seen: if detecting new relationships\n- Combination: if multiple factors matter\n\n**Example:** Threshold (count downloads) + filter (unusual browsers)\n\n### Step 4: Write the Query\n\n```sql\n-- Start with filtered data\nWITH filtered_events AS (\n  SELECT\n    actor.user.name AS user_name,\n    file_path,\n    http_request.user_agent AS user_agent,\n    time\n  FROM gold.file_activity\n  WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n    AND action = 'Download'\n    AND file_path LIKE '%confidential%'\n    AND http_request.user_agent NOT RLIKE '(?i)chrome|firefox|safari|edge'  -- Unusual browsers\n)\n-- Aggregate and apply threshold\nSELECT\n  user_name,\n  COUNT(*) AS download_count,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT file_path) AS unique_files,\n  COLLECT_SET(SUBSTRING(user_agent, 1, 50)) AS user_agents\nFROM filtered_events\nGROUP BY user_name\nHAVING download_count >= 5\nORDER BY download_count DESC\n```\n\n### Step 5: Validate and Refine\n\n**Verify:**\n\n- Does the query return expected results?\n- Are field names correct for your schema?\n- Are thresholds appropriate?\n- Does performance meet requirements?\n\n---\n\n## Common OCSF Fields\n\nLakewatch Gold tables typically follow the Open Cybersecurity Schema Framework (OCSF). Common fields include:\n\n### Authentication Events\n\n- `actor.user.name`, `actor.user.email`, `actor.user.uid`\n- `src_endpoint.ip`, `src_endpoint.hostname`, `src_endpoint.location.country`\n- `dst_endpoint.ip`, `dst_endpoint.hostname`\n- `status` (Success, Failure)\n- `activity_name` (Logon, Logoff, etc.)\n- `time`, `time_dt`\n\n### Network Activity\n\n- `src_endpoint.ip`, `dst_endpoint.ip`, `dst_endpoint.port`\n- `traffic.bytes`, `traffic.bytes_in`, `traffic.bytes_out`\n- `network_endpoint.port`, `network_endpoint.protocol`\n- `disposition` (Allowed, Blocked)\n\n### File Activity\n\n- `actor.user.name`\n- `file.path`, `file.name`, `file.size`\n- `action` (Create, Read, Write, Delete, Download, Upload)\n- `time`\n\n### HTTP/Web Activity\n\n- `http_request.http_method` (GET, POST, etc.)\n- `http_request.url.path`, `http_request.url.hostname`\n- `http_request.user_agent`\n- `http_response.code`\n\n### VPN Activity\n\n- `actor.user.name`\n- `src_endpoint.ip`, `src_endpoint.location.country`\n- `activity_name` (VPN Connect, VPN Disconnect)\n- `time`\n\n**Important:** Always verify field names in your specific Gold tables. Field availability varies by data source and ingestion configuration.\n\n---\n\n## SQL Best Practices for Detection\n\n### Time Window Filters\n\n**Always include explicit time ranges:**\n\n```sql\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n```\n\n**Why:** Improves query performance and prevents accidentally scanning entire table history.\n\n### Handling NULL Values\n\n**Use COALESCE for nullable fields:**\n\n```sql\nCOALESCE(http_request.user_agent, 'unknown') AS user_agent\n```\n\n**Why:** Prevents NULL values from breaking aggregations or causing unexpected results.\n\n### Aggregation with HAVING\n\n**Use HAVING for aggregated conditions:**\n\n```sql\nGROUP BY user_name\nHAVING COUNT(*) > 100  -- Correct\n-- Not: WHERE COUNT(*) > 100  -- Would fail\n```\n\n**Why:** WHERE filters before aggregation, HAVING filters after.\n\n### Use CTEs for Clarity\n\n**Break complex logic into CTEs:**\n\n```sql\nWITH filtered_data AS (\n  SELECT ... FROM ... WHERE ...\n),\naggregated_data AS (\n  SELECT ... FROM filtered_data GROUP BY ...\n)\nSELECT * FROM aggregated_data WHERE ...\n```\n\n**Why:** Easier to read, debug, and maintain.\n\n### Performance Considerations\n\n**Tips for faster queries:**\n\n- Always filter on partitioned columns (usually `time`)\n- Avoid SELECT \\* - only select needed columns\n- Use LIMIT during development/testing\n- Be cautious with cartesian joins (self-joins without proper conditions)\n\n---\n\n## Testing and Validation\n\n### Testing Your SQL\n\nBefore deploying a detection rule, test the SQL query:\n\n**1. Syntax Check**\n\n```sql\nEXPLAIN SELECT ...  -- Verify query plan\n```\n\n**2. Sample Results**\n\n```sql\nSELECT ... LIMIT 10  -- Check output format\n```\n\n**3. Count Check**\n\n```sql\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM (...) -- Verify result volume\n```\n\n**4. Time Range Verification**\n\n```sql\nSELECT MIN(time), MAX(time) FROM gold.your_table  -- Verify data availability\n```\n\n### Validating Detection Logic\n\nAfter initial deployment:\n\n**Check for False Positives:**\n\n- Review first 10-20 Notable events generated\n- Verify they represent actual suspicious activity\n- Adjust thresholds or filters if needed\n\n**Check for False Negatives:**\n\n- Manually query for known suspicious events\n- Verify they appear in detection results\n- Adjust detection logic if legitimate threats are missed\n\n**Monitor Performance:**\n\n- Check query execution time\n- Verify it completes within schedule interval\n- Optimize if queries are too slow\n\n**Tune Over Time:**\n\n- Track alert volume (too many = alert fatigue)\n- Adjust thresholds based on environment\n- Document changes for audit trail\n\n---\n\n## Common Time Window Patterns\n\n### Pattern 1: Fixed Interval from Now\n\n```sql\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n```\n\n**Use for:** Most batch detections (last X hours/days)\n\n### Pattern 2: Date-Based Windows\n\n```sql\nWHERE time BETWEEN DATE_SUB(CURRENT_DATE(), 1) AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n```\n\n**Use for:** Daily aggregations (yesterday's data)\n\n### Pattern 3: Specific Date Range\n\n```sql\nWHERE time BETWEEN '2025-01-01' AND '2025-01-31'\n```\n\n**Use for:** Historical analysis, backfilling\n\n### When to Use Each\n\n- **CURRENT_TIMESTAMP():** Real-time detection, rolling windows\n- **CURRENT_DATE():** Daily batch jobs, date-aligned aggregations\n- **Fixed dates:** Testing, historical investigations\n\n**Choose based on:**\n\n- Your detection schedule\n- Data arrival patterns\n- Business requirements (e.g., \"analyze yesterday's logins\")\n\n---\n\n## Example Detection Queries\n\n### Brute Force Attack\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  src_endpoint.ip AS attacker_ip,\n  actor.user.name AS target_user,\n  COUNT(*) AS failed_attempts,\n  MIN(time) AS attack_start,\n  MAX(time) AS attack_end\nFROM gold.authentication\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND status = 'Failure'\n  AND activity_name = 'Logon'\nGROUP BY src_endpoint.ip, actor.user.name\nHAVING failed_attempts > 20\n```\n\n### Data Exfiltration\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  SUM(traffic.bytes_out) / 1024 / 1024 AS mb_transferred,\n  COUNT(*) AS transfer_count\nFROM gold.network_activity\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND traffic.bytes_out > 0\nGROUP BY actor.user.name\nHAVING mb_transferred > 1000  -- Over 1 GB\n```\n\n### Privilege Escalation\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  target_user.name AS elevated_to,\n  time AS escalation_time\nFROM gold.authentication\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND activity_name = 'Assume Role'\n  AND target_user.name IN ('admin', 'root', 'Administrator')\n```\n\n### Lateral Movement\n\n```sql\nSELECT\n  actor.user.name AS user_name,\n  COUNT(DISTINCT dst_endpoint.hostname) AS unique_hosts_accessed,\n  COLLECT_SET(dst_endpoint.hostname) AS hosts\nFROM gold.authentication\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP()\n  AND activity_name = 'Logon'\n  AND status = 'Success'\nGROUP BY actor.user.name\nHAVING unique_hosts_accessed >= 5  -- Accessed 5+ different hosts\n```\n\n---\n\n## Troubleshooting\n\n**IMPORTANT:** As the AI agent, you should troubleshoot and fix errors BEFORE proposing SQL to the user. Use the guidance below to debug and correct issues automatically.\n\n### Query Returns No Results\n\n**When you encounter this during validation:**\n\n1. **Check data availability:**\n\n   ```sql\n   SELECT COUNT(*), MIN(time), MAX(time) FROM gold.your_table;\n   ```\n\n2. **Verify time window has data** - adjust if necessary\n3. **Remove filters incrementally** to identify which condition is too restrictive\n4. **Fix the query** and re-run before proposing to user\n\n**Debug approach:**\n\n```sql\n-- Step 1: Check if table has any data\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM gold.your_table;\n\n-- Step 2: Check if data exists in time window\nSELECT COUNT(*) FROM gold.your_table\nWHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 24 HOURS AND CURRENT_TIMESTAMP();\n\n-- Step 3: Add filters one by one to find the issue\n```\n\n### Field Not Found Errors\n\n**When you encounter \"field not found\" during validation:**\n\n**Automatic Fix Process:**\n\n1. **Use `tableSearch`** to find correct table names\n2. **Use `readTable`** to get actual schema and field names\n3. **Update SQL** with correct field names\n4. **Re-run** to validate\n5. **Only then propose** to user with explanation: \"I verified the correct field name is `X` in your schema\"\n\n**Example:**\n\n```\n❌ Error: \"Column 'src_endpoint.ip' not found\"\n→ Use readTable to check schema\n→ Find actual field: 'source.ip'\n→ Update SQL to use 'source.ip'\n→ Re-run and verify\n✅ Now propose corrected SQL to user\n```\n\n**Remember:** NEVER propose SQL with field names you haven't verified exist in the actual table schema.\n\n### Query Syntax Errors\n\n**When you encounter syntax errors during validation:**\n\n1. **Fix common issues:**\n   - Missing parentheses in CASE statements\n   - Incorrect date functions (use Spark SQL syntax)\n   - Wrong aggregate function usage\n\n2. **Re-run after each fix**\n3. **Don't tell the user about syntax errors** - fix them silently\n4. **Only present the working SQL**\n\n### Query Too Slow\n\n**If query takes too long during validation:**\n\n**Optimize automatically:**\n\n- Add/restrict time filter: `WHERE time BETWEEN CURRENT_TIMESTAMP() - INTERVAL 1 HOUR ...`\n- Remove SELECT \\* and specify only needed columns\n- Add LIMIT for initial testing\n- Simplify complex joins\n\n**Then re-run and verify performance is acceptable before proposing.**\n\n### Unexpected Results\n\n**Validate:**\n\n- Add LIMIT 10 to inspect sample rows\n- Check for NULL values in key fields\n- Verify aggregation logic with simple test cases\n- Compare against known events\n\n---\n\n## Summary\n\n**AI Agent Workflow (YOU MUST FOLLOW):**\n\n1. **Load this skill** when user requests SQL for detection\n2. **Generate SQL** based on appropriate pattern\n3. **Execute SQL** using `executeSql` to validate\n4. **If errors occur:**\n   - Use table discovery tools to verify table/field names\n   - Fix SQL syntax, fields, or filters\n   - Re-run until successful\n5. **Show validated results** to user with sample data\n6. **Only then propose** the working SQL for the detection rule\n\n**NEVER skip step 3 - Always validate SQL before proposing it to users.**\n\n---\n\n**Key Takeaways:**\n\n1. **Always validate SQL by running it** - Don't propose untested queries\n2. **Use explicit time filters** - Critical for performance\n3. **Verify field names** - Use table discovery tools to check schema\n4. **Start with templates** - Threshold, anomaly, first-time-seen, geo-patterns, time-based\n5. **Combine patterns** - Most effective detections use multiple techniques\n6. **Fix errors silently** - Debug and correct before showing to user\n7. **Test thoroughly** - Validate before deploying, tune after deployment\n8. **Use batch mode** - Recommended for most use cases\n\n**Next Steps:**\n\n1. Choose a detection pattern that matches your use case\n2. Identify the Gold table(s) containing your event data\n3. Verify field names using table discovery\n4. Adapt the pattern template to your specific requirements\n5. Test the query with LIMIT and sample data\n6. Deploy as a detection rule and monitor results\n\n**For More Information:**\n\n- **Threshold patterns:** See threshold-based detection skill\n- **Anomaly patterns:** See timeseries anomaly detection skill\n- **First-time-seen patterns:** See first-time-seen detection skill\n- **Detection rule configuration:** See detection rule documentation\n"}}]);
